Our fraud graph uses the Louvain community detection algorithm to generate exp_community_ring IDs based on shared PII nodes (email, phone, TIN). Analysts rely on these ring IDs as stable identifiers for ongoing investigations. However, during the next day’s data load, changes in PII-to-entity connections cause Louvain to produce different communities, resulting in new community ring IDs. This daily fluctuation breaks continuity, prevents analysts from tracking previous investigations, and leads to inconsistent cluster identification — a challenge known as Community ID Drift.





1----------------

You can achieve the “Case” pattern by adding one more vertex type and one relationship to your existing schema, and then rewiring only that new relationship after each Louvain run.​

Below is how it fits with your current model.

Current schema recap
From your description:

exp_user_ring → exp_community_ring (user belongs to a community ring)

exp_user_ring → PII nodes (Phone, Email, etc.)

exp_community_ring → PE_Entity

Louvain is effectively creating or updating exp_community_ring (or at least its IDs/labels).​

Add the Case layer
Introduce a new vertex:

Case (static, analyst-facing ID like CASE-001)

Add one relationship type:

(:Case)-[:FOCUSES_ON]->(:exp_community_ring)

Do not expose exp_community_ring.id to the analyst; only expose Case.case_id. The analyst always opens CASE-001; the backend resolves which exp_community_ring is currently linked.

So the full pattern becomes:

exp_user_ring → PII (Phone, Email, etc.)

exp_user_ring → exp_community_ring

exp_community_ring → PE_Entity

Case → exp_community_ring (new)

Day 1 example on your schema
Louvain run produces communities and you have rings: Ring_101, Ring_102, etc.​

Some exp_user_ring nodes point to Ring_101.

Analyst starts an investigation on that ring:

Create (:Case {case_id: 'CASE-001'}).

Create (:Case {case_id: 'CASE-001'})-[:FOCUSES_ON]->(:exp_community_ring {id:'Ring_101'}).

Now when the analyst opens CASE-001, backend does:

Find the ring:

MATCH (c:Case {case_id:'CASE-001'})-[:FOCUSES_ON]->(r:exp_community_ring)

Expand out to everything else:

Users: (u:exp_user_ring)-[:BELONGS_TO]->(r)

PII: (u)-[:HAS_PHONE|HAS_EMAIL]->(p)

Entities: (r)-[:LINKS_TO]->(e:PE_Entity)

So the analyst sees: all users, all PII, all PE_Entity associated with that community, but anchored by CASE-001, not by ring ID.

Day 2: rings change, Case stays
After a new Louvain run, suppose:

Old Ring_101 effectively “dies”.

New rings: Ring_201, Ring_202, etc.

Most of the users that were in Ring_101 are now in Ring_201.

Algorithm to rewire CASE-001:

Collect members of the old ring for CASE-001:

Get u_old = { all exp_user_ring that pointed to Ring_101 }.

For each new ring R_new (Ring_201, Ring_202, …), get its members u_new.

Compute overlap:

overlap(R_new) = |u_old ∩ u_new|. ​

Take R_best = ring with max overlap and above a threshold (e.g., overlap ≥ 60% of u_old).

Update just the Case edge:

Delete (:Case {CASE-001})-[:FOCUSES_ON]->(:exp_community_ring {Ring_101}).

Create (:Case {CASE-001})-[:FOCUSES_ON]->(:exp_community_ring {Ring_201}).

You do not touch:

exp_user_ring → PII

exp_community_ring → PE_Entity

Those are regenerated/relinked by your Louvain pipeline as usual; only the Case → exp_community_ring pointer is swapped to the “best matching” new ring.

Why this avoids ring ID collision
exp_community_ring IDs can change every run, even be completely dropped and recreated, because the analyst never bookmarks them.​

The analyst’s stable anchor is Case.case_id.

After each Louvain run, backend recomputes “which new ring best represents this Case?” using user overlap, and re-attaches the Case to that ring.

