Core idea: “The Ring ID follows the Center of Gravity”
Instead of letting Louvain assign a fresh, random community ID every day, you:

Keep a stable ring ID on each PE_Entity (or exp_user_ring) that survives reloads.​

After each Louvain run, you vote inside each new cluster: “Which stable ring ID do most of the nodes already have?”

You then overwrite the stable ring ID of all nodes in that cluster with the winning ID (the “center of gravity”).

From the analyst’s view, the ring ID doesn’t flicker; it just grows or shrinks as nodes join/leave.​

Step 1: Add the stable ring ID attribute
On your main entity node (whichever is the “core” node Louvain runs on), add a persistent attribute:

On PE_Entity (or exp_user_ring, if that’s Louvain’s node):

Add a property: stable_ring_id (e.g., RING-A, RING-B, RING-C).​

This stable_ring_id:

Is never deleted when you reload data (unlike Louvain’s temporary community ID).

Is only updated by the PID propagation logic after each Louvain run.

Example:

text
// Example node (PE_Entity)
(:PE_Entity {
  entity_id: 'E123',
  stable_ring_id: 'RING-A'  // This is the PID
})
Step 2: Daily pipeline with PID propagation
Step A: Load & Link (Day 2)
Load new data (new exp_user_ring, new PII, new PE_Entity, etc.).​

Link them as usual:

exp_user_ring → exp_community_ring (temporary, from Louvain)

exp_user_ring → PII (Phone, Email, etc.)

exp_community_ring → PE_Entity

At this point, new nodes have stable_ring_id: null (or some default like NEW), and old nodes still have their old stable_ring_id (e.g., RING-A).​

Step B: Run Louvain
Run Louvain on your graph (on exp_user_ring or PE_Entity, depending on your model).​

Louvain outputs a temporary community ID on each node:

Add a temporary property: temp_louvain_id (e.g., TEMP-101, TEMP-102, …).

Now each node has:

text
(:PE_Entity {
  entity_id: 'E123',
  stable_ring_id: 'RING-A',
  temp_louvain_id: 'TEMP-101'
})
Step C: The “Vote” (PID Propagation)
This is the “magic” step that makes ring IDs stable.​

1. Group by new Louvain cluster
For each new cluster (each temp_louvain_id), collect all its nodes and their old stable_ring_id.​

Example for temp_louvain_id = TEMP-101:

Node	stable_ring_id
E123	RING-A
E456	RING-A
E789	RING-A
E101	RING-B
E202	RING-B
2. Count votes per stable_ring_id
Inside this cluster, count how many nodes had each stable_ring_id:

RING-A: 3 votes

RING-B: 2 votes

The majority (center of gravity) is RING-A.​

3. Update stable_ring_id for the whole cluster
Overwrite stable_ring_id for all nodes in this cluster to the winning ID:

text
// Pseudocode
UPDATE PE_Entity
SET stable_ring_id = 'RING-A'
WHERE temp_louvain_id = 'TEMP-101'
Now all 5 nodes in TEMP-101 have stable_ring_id = 'RING-A'.​

Repeat this for every temp_louvain_id cluster.

Step 3: How this handles real-world cases
Case 1: Ring A absorbs Ring B (Merge)
Old:

Ring A: 100 nodes with stable_ring_id = 'RING-A'

Ring B: 5 nodes with stable_ring_id = 'RING-B'

New Louvain cluster: one big cluster with 105 nodes (A + B).

Vote inside this cluster:

100 votes for RING-A

5 votes for RING-B

Winner: RING-A

Result: all 105 nodes now have stable_ring_id = 'RING-A'

Analyst sees: “Ring A” still exists, and now it has 105 members instead of 100.​

Case 2: Ring A splits into big and small chunks
Old: Ring A (100 nodes, stable_ring_id = 'RING-A')

New Louvain:

Cluster 1: 90 nodes (mostly from old Ring A)

Cluster 2: 10 nodes (minority from old Ring A)

Cluster 1 (90 nodes):

90 votes for RING-A → winner is RING-A

All 90 nodes keep stable_ring_id = 'RING-A'

Cluster 2 (10 nodes):

10 votes for RING-A, no other ID dominates

Decision rule:

If a threshold is met (e.g., ≥ 60% of cluster votes for one ID), keep that ID.

Else, assign a new stable ring ID (e.g., RING-C).

So Cluster 2 might become RING-C.​

Analyst sees:

Ring A still exists (the big chunk).

A new ring (Ring C) appears for the small split-off group.

Case 3: New ring (no dominant old ID)
New cluster with 50 nodes:

20 from old Ring A

20 from old Ring B

10 new nodes (no old stable_ring_id)

Vote: no clear majority (20 vs 20).

Decision: assign a new stable ring ID (e.g., RING-D).

Analyst sees: a new ring RING-D appears.​

Step 4: How the analyst uses it
The analyst never sees temp_louvain_id or the raw Louvain cluster ID.​

Instead, they always query by stable_ring_id:

text
// Analyst query: "Show me everything in RING-A"
MATCH (e:PE_Entity {stable_ring_id: 'RING-A'})
OPTIONAL MATCH (e)-[:LINKS_TO]->(ring:exp_community_ring)
OPTIONAL MATCH (ring)<-[:BELONGS_TO]-(user:exp_user_ring)
OPTIONAL MATCH (user)-[:HAS_PHONE|HAS_EMAIL]->(pii)
RETURN e, ring, user, collect(pii) AS pii_list
Result:

The analyst always sees RING-A as the same “golden record.”

Behind the scenes, the nodes in RING-A may have changed (new members joined, some left), but the ID stays stable.​

Why this is the “best” solution for your use case
No ring ID collision: Louvain can assign any temp_louvain_id you want; the analyst only sees the stable stable_ring_id.​

Analyst trust: The ring ID doesn’t flicker; it behaves like a persistent case or investigation.

Merges & splits handled naturally: The majority vote automatically preserves the “main” ring and spawns new IDs only when a minority chunk is truly distinct.

Works with your schema: You keep exp_community_ring, exp_user_ring, PII, and PE_Entity as-is; you just add stable_ring_id and a post‑Louvain voting step.​

If you share which graph DB (Neo4j, TigerGraph, etc.) and which language (Cypher, GSQL, etc.) you use, a concrete code template for the voting step can be written for your stack




