Step 1: Snapshot PEEntity → Old Ring ID
Just before you wipe exp_community_ring, take a minimal snapshot of yesterday’s community intelligence.

Data to export (example):

PE_Entity_id

old_ring_id (yesterday’s exp_community_ring UUID or some stable_ring_id)

Conceptually:

Query: “For each PE_Entity, which ring was it in yesterday?”

Store the result into:

A staging table in your warehouse (fraud_pe_entity_ring_snapshot), or

A flat file (CSV/Parquet) keyed by PE_Entity_id.

Example snapshot table:

PE_Entity_id	old_ring_id
E1	RING_123
E2	RING_123
E3	RING_456
This is your “template” of yesterday’s world.

Step 2: Nuclear Wipe and Fresh Load
Now completely reset the Louvain side of the graph.

Delete:

All exp_community_ring nodes and their edges (to exp_user_ring, PE_Entity, etc.).

Load today’s fresh transactional data:

New exp_user_ring

PII nodes / edges (phone, email, TIN)

PE_Entity

Benefit: The graph has no stale or dangling community edges; Louvain will see a clean snapshot of today’s reality.​

Step 3: Run Louvain to Create New Rings
Run Louvain again on today’s graph (likely on exp_user_ring or PE_Entity, depending on your model).​

Louvain generates new community IDs (e.g., Ring_X, Ring_Y) and you materialize them as new exp_community_ring nodes.

Also create / maintain:

exp_user_ring → exp_community_ring edges

exp_community_ring → PE_Entity edges

At this moment, all community IDs are “fresh” and have no continuity relation to yesterday’s IDs.

Step 4: Restore Old IDs by Overlap (“Rename” Rings)
Now you reuse the snapshot to give each new ring the old ID whose members it mostly inherited.

4.1 Compute overlap per new ring
For each new exp_community_ring (say Ring_X):

Collect its member PE_Entity IDs:

From today’s graph: Ring_X → PE_Entity list.

Join this list with your snapshot (Step 1) on PE_Entity_id:

For each PE_Entity in Ring_X, look up old_ring_id from yesterday.

Count how many entities in Ring_X came from each old_ring_id.

Example:

Today’s Ring_X has entities: {E1, E2, E3, E4}

Snapshot:

E1 → RING_123

E2 → RING_123

E3 → RING_123

E4 → RING_789

Vote counts for Ring_X:

RING_123: 3

RING_789: 1

So the “best matching” old ring ID for Ring_X is RING_123.

4.2 Decide: reuse or create new ID
For each new ring:

If one old_ring_id has a strong majority (e.g., ≥ 60–70% of this new ring’s members), reuse that ID.

Example: Ring_X → rename to RING_123.

If there is no clear majority (e.g., heavily mixed from many old rings or a lot of brand‑new entities), assign a new ID.

Example: Ring_Y → keep its new UUID or assign RING_NEW_001.

4.3 Apply the rename
You have two options depending on your implementation:

Option A (preferred conceptually):

Treat ring_id as a property on exp_community_ring.

Set exp_community_ring.ring_id = reused_old_id_or_new_id.

Option B:

If you truly use the node UUID as the community ID, you can:

Either store the “business ID” as a separate property (business_ring_id) used in the UI, or

Recreate the exp_community_ring nodes with the desired IDs and rewire edges (heavier but conceptually same).

From the analyst’s perspective:

Yesterday they saw RING_123.

Today, RING_123 still exists, but its membership is slightly changed because the world changed.

Community ID drift is drastically reduced: the “name” of the ring is carried forward to the new cluster that best matches yesterday’s ring.​

Why this fits your fraud graph and when to use it
Works directly with your model:

Louvain over shared PII on exp_user_ring / PE_Entity → exp_community_ring rings.​

The snapshot is just PE_Entity → old ring ID; no schema change needed.

Strong benefits:

Zero complex incremental logic; every day is “delete, rebuild, then restore names.”

Self‑healing if yesterday’s graph was corrupted, because you recompute everything from scratch.

Trade‑offs:

During the reload and Louvain run, the community layer is “offline” for analysts.

For very large graphs, full rebuild + overlap join can be computationally heavy.​

But conceptually it gives your analysts what they want: ring labels that are stable over time, even though the underlying Louvain communities are recomputed from scratch each day.
